Z||000000||sqoach||06-29-2001||09:23 PM||||Anyone know the part #? Is the V6 filter smaller than the 4 cylinder?||||reg||1
Z||000001||WMEBERT||06-29-2001||10:00 PM||||The Fram Number is:  PH3980    I'm not sure about the size of the 4 cylinder filter compared to the V6.  Visit your local parts store and see.<P>Bill<BR>86 GT||||reg||3
Z||000002||WMEBERT||06-29-2001||10:06 PM||||Sorry---Wrong Info.  I just bought 2 Fram Oil Filters-one for my GT and one for the wifes Blazer.  I looked at the wrong receipt. The CORRECT Fram number for the 2.8 V6 is PH3387A   I verified this both on AutoZone.com and Advanceautoparts.com.<P>Hope this helps......<P>Bill<BR>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||06-29-2001||10:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WMEBERT:<BR><B>Sorry---Wrong Info.  I just bought 2 Fram Oil Filters-one for my GT and one for the wifes Blazer.  I looked at the wrong receipt. The CORRECT Fram number for the 2.8 V6 is PH3387A   I verified this both on AutoZone.com and Advanceautoparts.com.<P>Hope this helps......<P>Bill<BR>86 GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, helps bunches. I went to two places and they showed the 3387. But on the car was the 3980, which is a little bigger, so I was confused. Will it hurt to have the larger one on there? I bought both. Should I use the correct one?||||reg||1
Z||000004||WMEBERT||06-29-2001||10:40 PM||||Thats your call.  If you had the PH3980 on before with no problems you should be okay.  The PH3387A is probably recommended because of "fitment" issues.  I know the small block Chevy has a few filters that will fit with the only difference being what car/truck it's installed in. The larger filter will obviously give you more filtering capability plus slightly more oil capacity.  <P>Incidently...I am a Division Manager for Advance Auto Parts with 20 years experience in the Automotive market so I have a "little" hands on experience in the field.  (I was quite embarrased when I posted the wrong part number in my 1st post <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  ) <P>Good Luck,<P>Bill<BR>86 GT||||reg||6
Z||000005||sqoach||06-29-2001||10:45 PM||||Actually, I just picked up this car. Just doing some tune-up maintenance. I figure it couldn't hurt to have the little bit bigger filter. But that's me.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||WMEBERT||06-29-2001||10:54 PM||||I justed checked advanceautoparts.com<BR>They list the PH3980 for the 84 Fiero with the Iron Duke 4 cylinder.  I'd compare the filters closely before installing....are the treads the same, does the gasket (o-ring) seem to be the same size?  If you do take a chance with the PH3980 I'd be sure to check for leaks upon starting it up after the oil change.  Good Luck....<P><BR>Bill<BR>86 GT||||reg||6
Z||000007||mrfiero||06-29-2001||10:59 PM||||The 3387A was the original oil filter that was recommended for the V-6 Fiero (this was the one I used when I bought my first GT 11 years ago).<P>However, several years ago they upgraded the filter recomendation to the 3980 (AC Delco 52).  This adds about 1/2 quart of oil plus it's easier to put on/take off.<P>Use the bigger 3980 filter!  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||WMEBERT||06-29-2001||11:17 PM||||Great Info Mr. Fiero.  I'll use the PH3980 next time.<P>Bill<BR>86 GT<P>You're never too old to learn and do I feel old!  I'll be 40 on Tuesday!||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfiero||06-29-2001||11:59 PM||||No problem......some books still list the 3387A, but others list the newer recomendation, so it's easy to get confused.  <P>I'm pushing 30, so you have me beat!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||sqoach||06-30-2001||12:29 PM||||I put the 3980 on, and taking the other one back. This may be a dumb question, but how much oil do I use? I put in 4 quarts, and now it's showing really high oil pressure on my guage. Use to be just above 40. I didn't think 4 would be too much. My sensor might be bad, though.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||06-30-2001||01:00 PM||||Regardless of brand ALL 84 4 cyl get the long PF52/xxx3980 (AC/Fram) filter. Always. This is a recall change item. Most parts books should list it that way. (The recall listed PF51 is replaced by the PF52)<P>The pf47/xxx3387 is listed for all others. Officially this is the only filter you should use.... (Except 88 4 cyl with pan filter... you don't get a choice.)<P>Here's the catch.... not all lines of filters carry the short body filter. Last time I looked at the castrol catalog they spec'd the long body filter for all 4 cyl and I think V6. (It's been awhile since I last looked...)<P>The long and short filter are identical on the head end. There is no danger there.<P>Putting the long filter on slightly increases oil capacity but you have to be carefull how you look at that... this is a trapped volume. While more oil is circulating, pan volume is unchanged. You'll still be low at the same pan depth as with the short filter. (In other words... The 84 is still considered low 1/2 quart less than full.)<P>It's also easier to change the long filter on the 4 cyl. (I have a hell of a time reaching the long one, the short one is almost imposible.) On the V6 make sure you have enough room. there is just enough variation that it doesn't always have enough clearance on some cars.<P>Why did you put xxx in the fram number... because fram uses the xxx space to describe which product group the filter is. These numbers are also used by many house brands and others like Castrol. The letter part changes but the number part is the same for all brands that use this number system.||||reg||1
Z||000012||KRMFiero||06-30-2001||09:49 PM||||4-2.5 F/inj. (R)<BR>Extra Guard:<BR>PH3980<BR>Tough Guard:<BR>TG3980<BR>Double Guard:<BR>DG3980<P>V6-2.8 F/inj. (9)<BR>Extra Guard:<BR>PH3387A<BR>Tough Guard:<BR>TG3387A<BR>Double Guard:<BR>DG3387A||||reg||1
